<worksheet>
    <title>LT 17 Quadratic Regression</title>
<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      A professor surveys five students in a college algebra course. Each student reports how many hours they studied for the midterm exam, and the professor records their exam score. Studying helps, but students who studied for very long stretches often reported being tired on exam day.
    </p>
    <table xml:id="tbl-study-scores">
      <title>Study time and midterm exam score</title>
      <tabular halign="center" top="medium" bottom="medium">
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Hours studied, <m>x</m></cell>
          <cell>1</cell>
          <cell>2</cell>
          <cell>3</cell>
          <cell>4</cell>
          <cell>5</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Exam score, <m>y</m> (out of 100)</cell>
          <cell>62</cell>
          <cell>74</cell>
          <cell>81</cell>
          <cell>83</cell>
          <cell>80</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </introduction>

  <task workspace="0.01in">
    <statement>
      <p>Identify the independent and dependent variables. Then make a scatter plot of the data. Does a linear model seem appropriate? Why might a quadratic model make more sense than a linear model here?</p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace="0.01in">
    <statement>
      <p>Use technology to find the quadratic regression equation <m>y = ax^2 + bx + c</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p><m>y = -2.5x^2 + 19.5x + 45</m></p>
    </answer>
  </task>

  <task workspace="0.01in">
    <statement>
      <p>Use your model to predict the exam score of a student who studies for 3.5 hours. Answer in a complete sentence.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>About 82.6 points.</p>
    </answer>
  </task>

  <task workspace="0.01in"> 
    <statement>
      <p>According to your model, how many hours of studying give the highest exam score, and what is that score? Answer in a complete sentence.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>The vertex is at <m>x = 3.9</m> hours, where the predicted score is about 83.0 points.</p>
    </answer>
  </task>

  <task workspace="0.01in">
    <statement>
      <p>You want to earn at least an 80 on the exam. Set up and solve an equation to find the range of study times for which the model predicts a score of at least 80. Answer in a complete sentence.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Solving <m>-2.5x^2 + 19.5x + 45 = 80</m> gives <m>x = 2.8</m> and <m>x = 5</m>. The model predicts a score of at least 80 for studying between 2.8 and 5 hours.</p>
    </answer>
  </task>

  <task workspace="0.01in">
    <statement>
      <p>What does the value of <m>c</m> represent in this context? Does that prediction seem reasonable? What other factors (sleep, prior knowledge, test anxiety) might this model leave out?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>The value <m>c = 45</m> is the predicted score for a student who studies 0 hours. It's plausible that someone could earn some points without studying, but the model is based on data from 1 to 5 hours, so it's an extrapolation.</p>
    </answer>
  </task>
</exercise>
</worksheet>